            {
              "id": "HS-JAVASCRIPT-6",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "(1/1) * Possible vulnerability detected: No use weak random number generator\nWhen software generates predictable values in a context requiring unpredictability, it may be possible for an attacker to guess the next value that will be generated, and use this guess to impersonate another user or access sensitive information. As the Math.random() function relies on a weak pseudorandom number generator, this function should not be used for security-critical applications or for protecting sensitive data. In such context, a cryptographically strong pseudorandom number generator (CSPRNG) should be used instead. For more information checkout the CWE-338 (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html) advisory."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "(1/1) * Possible vulnerability detected: No use weak random number generator\nWhen software generates predictable values in a context requiring unpredictability, it may be possible for an attacker to guess the next value that will be generated, and use this guess to impersonate another user or access sensitive information. As the Math.random() function relies on a weak pseudorandom number generator, this function should not be used for security-critical applications or for protecting sensitive data. In such context, a cryptographically strong pseudorandom number generator (CSPRNG) should be used instead. For more information checkout the CWE-338 (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html) advisory."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.horusec.io/docs/cli/analysis-tools/overview/",
              "name": "(1/1) * Possible vulnerability detected: No use weak random number generator"
            },
            {
              "id": "HS-JAVASCRIPT-21",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "(1/1) * Possible vulnerability detected: Using command line arguments\nCommand line arguments can be dangerous just like any other user input. They should never be used without being first validated and sanitized. Remember also that any user can retrieve the list of processes running on a system, which makes the arguments provided to them visible. Thus passing sensitive information via command line arguments should be considered as insecure. This rule raises an issue when on every program entry points (main methods) when command line arguments are used. The goal is to guide security code reviews. Sanitize all command line arguments before using them. For more information checkout the CWE-88 (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/88.html) advisory."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "(1/1) * Possible vulnerability detected: Using command line arguments\nCommand line arguments can be dangerous just like any other user input. They should never be used without being first validated and sanitized. Remember also that any user can retrieve the list of processes running on a system, which makes the arguments provided to them visible. Thus passing sensitive information via command line arguments should be considered as insecure. This rule raises an issue when on every program entry points (main methods) when command line arguments are used. The goal is to guide security code reviews. Sanitize all command line arguments before using them. For more information checkout the CWE-88 (https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/88.html) advisory."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://docs.horusec.io/docs/cli/analysis-tools/overview/",
              "name": "(1/1) * Possible vulnerability detected: Using command line arguments"
            },
